<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/handshake.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/handshake-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/handshake.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Neutral Umpire Services</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Appraisal service</p><h1>Neutral Insurance Appraisal Umpire in Michigan</h1><p class="ph-lede">When two appraisers can’t agree on the amount of loss, an impartial umpire breaks the deadlock. We serve as a neutral umpire statewide, by agreement or court appointment.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-maize" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-glass" href="/request-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Appointment</dt><dd>Agreed by appraisers or court</dd></div><div><dt>Selection window</dt><dd>15 days under the statute</dd></div><div><dt>Award</dt><dd>Signed by any two of three</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>The umpire’s role in Michigan appraisal</h2>
<p>Under the appraisal framework in Michigan’s Insurance Code (MCL 500.2833), each side chooses its own appraiser, and the two appraisers then select an umpire. In general terms, they have 15 days to agree on one; if they can’t, a court of record in the state can be asked to select an umpire. The umpire doesn’t act for either side. When the appraisers can’t agree on items, they submit their differences to the umpire, and a written award signed by any two of the three determines the amount of loss.</p>
<h2>Neutral by design</h2>
<p>When we serve as an umpire, we are not anyone’s advocate. We won’t serve as umpire in a dispute where we’ve acted as appraiser or estimate reviewer for either party, and we disclose any prior relationship with the parties, appraisers or insurer before accepting an appointment. Neutrality is the whole point; without it, the process loses its value.</p>
<h2>How we get involved</h2>
<h3>Agreed appointment</h3>
<p>Most often, the two appraisers agree on an umpire directly. Appraisers may contact us with the claim basics so we can check for conflicts, confirm availability and outline fee terms before both sides sign off.</p>
<h3>Court appointment</h3>
<p>When appraisers can’t agree, either party may ask a Michigan court of record to select an umpire. Courts sometimes consider candidates suggested by the parties. We can provide a statement of qualifications and conflict disclosure for that purpose. The decision belongs to the court.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Umpires decide amounts, not coverage</b>Like the appraisers, an umpire resolves the amount of loss. Questions about whether an item is covered, about penalty interest or about unfair claim practices belong to the parties, their attorneys and, if needed, a court.</div>
<h2>What the process looks like</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Intake and disclosures.</strong> We confirm the parties, review for conflicts, and agree on fee terms and how they’re shared, as the policy provides.</li>
<li><strong>Submissions.</strong> Each appraiser provides an estimate, photos and supporting documents, usually with a list of items already agreed.</li>
<li><strong>Site inspection.</strong> Where useful, we inspect the property, often with both appraisers present.</li>
<li><strong>Meeting.</strong> We hear each appraiser’s position on disputed items, ask questions and request more support where needed.</li>
<li><strong>Award.</strong> We work toward a written award signed by two of the three, typically itemized so the parties can see how the amount was reached.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Keeping awards clear</h2>
<p>A well-drafted award reduces later disputes. It typically identifies the property and date of loss, separates replacement cost and actual cash value where the policy calls for it, and notes items that were left for coverage decisions. An umpire shouldn’t decide coverage, so clear itemization helps the parties apply the award to their policy.</p>
<p>Learn more about each step in the <a href="/michigan-appraisal-process/">Michigan appraisal process</a>, see definitions in the <a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">glossary</a>, or browse <a href="/michigan-appraisal-questions/">common appraisal questions</a>. We serve as umpires on residential and commercial losses across both peninsulas. We are not a law firm and give no legal advice.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>How is an umpire chosen in a Michigan appraisal?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>The two appraisers generally have 15 days to agree on an umpire. If they can’t, a court of record in Michigan can be asked to select one.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Who pays the umpire?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Many policies split the umpire’s fee equally between the insured and the insurer. Check your policy’s appraisal clause for its specific terms.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Can an umpire decide whether something is covered?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. The umpire helps set the amount of loss only. Coverage questions remain for the parties, their attorneys or a court.</p></div></details></div>
